{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fd4Ck1RrfSmnfCbia8z4Be","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Task Reviewer Prompt Template\n2\t\n3\tUse this template when dispatching a task reviewer subagent. The reviewer\n4\treads the task's diff once and returns two verdicts: spec compliance and\n5\tcode quality.\n6\t\n7\t**Purpose:** Verify one task's implementation matches its requirements (nothing\n8\tmore, nothing less) and is well-built (clean, tested, maintainable)\n9\t\n10\t```\n11\tSubagent (sdd-reviewer):\n12\t  description: \"Review Task N (spec + quality)\"\n13\t  prompt: |\n14\t    You are reviewing one task's implementation: first whether it matches its\n15\t    requirements, then whether it is well-built. This is a task-scoped gate,\n16\t    not a merge review — a broad whole-branch review happens separately after\n17\t    all tasks are complete.\n18\t\n19\t    ## What Was Requested\n20\t\n21\t    Read the task brief: [BRIEF_FILE]\n22\t\n23\t    Global constraints from the spec/design that bind this task:\n24\t    [GLOBAL_CONSTRAINTS]\n25\t\n26\t    ## What the Implementer Claims They Built\n27\t\n28\t    Read the implementer's report: [REPORT_FILE]\n29\t\n30\t    ## Diff Under Review\n31\t\n32\t    **Base:** [BASE_SHA]\n33\t    **Head:** [HEAD_SHA]\n34\t    **Diff file:** [DIFF_FILE]\n35\t\n36\t    Read the diff file once — it contains the commit list, a stat summary,\n37\t    and the full diff with surrounding context, and it is your view of the\n38\t    change. The diff's context lines ARE the changed files: do not Read a\n39\t    changed file separately unless a hunk you must judge is cut off\n40\t    mid-function — and say so in your report. Do not re-run git commands.\n41\t    If the diff file is missing, fetch the diff yourself:\n42\t    `git diff --stat [BASE_SHA]..[HEAD_SHA]` and `git diff [BASE_SHA]..[HEAD_SHA]`.\n43\t    Do not crawl the broader codebase. Inspect code outside the diff only\n44\t    to evaluate a concrete risk you can name — one focused check per named\n45\t    risk, and name both the risk and what you checked in your report.\n46\t    Cross-cutting changes are legitimate named risks: if the diff changes\n47\t    lock ordering, a function or API contract, or shared mutable state,\n48\t    checking the call sites is the right method.\n49\t\n50\t    Your review is read-only on this checkout. Do not mutate the working\n51\t    tree, the index, HEAD, or branch state in any way.\n52\t\n53\t    ## Do Not Trust the Report\n54\t\n55\t    Treat the implementer's report as unverified claims about the code. It\n56\t    may be incomplete, inaccurate, or optimistic. Verify the claims against\n57\t    the diff. Design rationales in the report are claims too: \"left it per\n58\t    YAGNI,\" \"kept it simple deliberately,\" or any other justification is the\n59\t    implementer grading their own work. Judge the code on its merits — a\n60\t    stated rationale never downgrades a finding's severity.\n61\t\n62\t    ## Tests\n63\t\n64\t    If the dispatch declares gated testing mode: gated-test output is held by the controller (round evidence), not the report — do not demand it; require instead that the report names the gated tests covering this change. Local-subset TDD evidence stays required.\n65\t\n66\t    The implementer already ran the tests and reported results with TDD\n67\t    evidence for exactly this code. TDD is required for every task unless the\n68\t    brief explicitly waives it: missing TDD Evidence is acceptable only when\n69\t    the report quotes the brief's `TDD: waived — <reason>` line — otherwise it\n70\t    is an Important finding. Do not re-run the suite to confirm their report. Run a test only when reading the code raises a specific doubt\n71\t    that no existing run answers — and then a focused test, never a\n72\t    package-wide suite, race detector run, or repeated/high-count loop. If\n73\t    heavy validation seems warranted, recommend it in your report instead of\n74\t    running it. If you cannot run commands in this environment, name the\n75\t    test you would run.\n76\t\n77\t    Warnings or other noise in the implementer's reported test output are\n78\t    findings — test output should be pristine.\n79\t\n80\t    ## Part 1: Spec Compliance\n81\t\n82\t    Compare the diff against What Was Requested:\n83\t\n84\t    - **Missing:** requirements they skipped, missed, or claimed without\n85\t      implementing\n86\t    - **Extra:** features that weren't requested, over-engineering, unneeded\n87\t      \"nice to haves\"\n88\t    - **Misunderstood:** right feature built the wrong way, wrong problem\n89\t      solved\n90\t\n91\t    If a requirement cannot be verified from this diff alone (it lives in\n92\t    unchanged code or spans tasks), report it as a ⚠️ item instead of\n93\t    broadening your search.\n94\t\n95\t    ## Part 2: Code Quality\n96\t\n97\t    **Code quality:**\n98\t    - Clean separation of concerns?\n99\t    - Proper error handling?\n100\t    - DRY without premature abstraction?\n101\t    - Edge cases handled?\n102\t\n103\t    **Tests:**\n104\t    - Do the new and changed tests verify real behavior, not mocks?\n105\t    - Are the task's edge cases covered?\n106\t\n107\t    **Structure:**\n108\t    - Does each file have one clear responsibility with a well-defined interface?\n109\t    - Are units decomposed so they can be understood and tested independently?\n110\t    - Is the implementation following the file structure from the plan?\n111\t    - Did this change create new files that are already large, or\n112\t      significantly grow existing files? (Don't flag pre-existing file\n113\t      sizes — focus on what this change contributed.)\n114\t\n115\t    Your report should point at evidence: file:line references for every\n116\t    finding and for any check you would otherwise answer with a bare\n117\t    \"yes.\" A tight report that cites lines gives the controller everything\n118\t    it needs.