{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CdYd3SCN7QHR8N6oRt8oo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"### Spec Compliance\n\nMatches the brief exactly: `load_rows(conn, name: str, rows: list[list[str]]) -> int` lives in `rows.py`, is exercised by a failing-then-passing test in `tests/test_rows.py`, and the returned count is asserted against the number of inserted rows. No cross-task coupling: `tinycsv.py` and `tests/test_tinycsv.py` are untouched, matching \"Consumes: nothing.\" Nothing missing, nothing extra beyond the signature requested.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- Real DB coverage: test uses an actual `sqlite3` connection via `tinycsv.connect` against a `tmp_path` file, not a mock, and asserts both the return value and the row count read back from the table.\n- Genuine TDD evidence: RED run shows `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'rows'` before the file existed; GREEN run shows both tests passing.\n- Tight scope: 10-line implementation, no speculative batching/validation/type coercion beyond what the brief asked for.\n- No scope creep into sibling files.\n\n### Issues (Critical/Important/Minor)\n\n**Important**\n- `f\"INSERT INTO {name} VALUES ({placeholders})\"` interpolates the table name with no validation, escaping, or quoting at all. The implementer's framing (\"parameter binding only covers values, not identifiers, so this is standard\") is correct as far as it goes — sqlite3 truly can't bind identifiers with `?`. But that only explains why some interpolation is unavoidable; it doesn't justify skipping the cheap follow-up step of validating the identifier (e.g. `name.isidentifier()`) before use. Given this repo's stated purpose is a CSV-to-SQLite loader, `name` derived from a filename or CSV header is a very plausible future call site, and nothing in this diff or the brief documents \"caller must pre-validate `name`\" as a contract. Not exploitable from anything in this diff (the only caller is the