{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Pye23q49mkX5J2KEvCpqVw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Task 3 report: load_rows\n2\t\n3\t## Implemented\n4\t`rows.py`: `load_rows(conn, name: str, rows: list[list[str]]) -> int`.\n5\tBuilds an `INSERT INTO {name} VALUES (?,...)` statement sized to the width of the\n6\tfirst row, runs it via `conn.executemany`, commits, and returns `len(rows)`.\n7\tEmpty `rows` list returns `0` without touching the DB.\n8\t\n9\tNo validation, type coercion, or batching beyond a single `executemany` call —\n10\tkept to what the brief and YAGNI require.\n11\t\n12\t## TDD evidence\n13\t\n14\tRED:\n15\t```\n16\t$ uv run --no-project python -m pytest -q tests/test_rows.py\n17\tERROR collecting tests/test_rows.py\n18\tModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'rows'\n19\t1 error in 0.06s\n20\t```\n21\t\n22\tGREEN:\n23\t```\n24\t$ uv run --no-project python -m pytest -q\n25\t..                                                                       [100%]\n26\t2 passed in 0.04s\n27\t```\n28\t(2 = the new test_rows.py test + the pre-existing test_tinycsv.py test, both pass, no warnings.)\n29\t\n30\t## Files changed\n31\t- `rows.py` (new)\n32\t- `tests/test_rows.py` (new)\n33\t- Did not touch `tinycsv.py` or `tests/test_tinycsv.py`.\n34\t\n35\t## Test design note\n36\tThe test creates its own target table directly with raw SQL\n37\t(`CREATE TABLE t (a, b)`) via the real sqlite3 connection returned by\n38\t`tinycsv.connect`, per the brief's guidance to avoid depending on the sibling\n39\t`create_table` task in `schema.py`. It asserts both the returned count and the\n40\tactual row count in the table (`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t`), which stays within\n41\tscope — it verifies the one behavior the function has, not extra scenarios.\n42\t\n43\t## Self-review\n44\t- Completeness: brief's two steps (failing test, then implementation) both done, matching the exact signature and file names specified.\n45\t- Quality: single small function, no unused imports, docstring present per repo convention.\n46\t- YAGNI: no schema introspection, no column-name handling, no error handling for row-width mismatches or SQL injection on table name — none of these were requested and the brief describes \"a simple insert-and-count.\"\n47\t- Test hygiene: pristine `pytest -q` output, no warnings, no skipped tests, no dependency on sibling tasks' files.\n48\t\n49\t## Concerns\n50\t- `name` (table name) is interpolated directly into the SQL string with an f-string. This is standard for identifiers in sqlite3 (parameter binding only covers values, not identifiers) and is consistent with how a minimal loader would call this internally with a trusted, already-validated table name — not an issue for this task's scope, but worth knowing if `name` ever becomes user-supplied without validation upstream.\n51\t- Row width is taken from `rows[0]`; if callers ever pass rows of inconsistent length, `executemany` will raise a sqlite3 error naturally — no explicit handling added, per YAGNI.\n52\t\n53\t---\n54\t\n55\t## Review fix: table-name validation in `load_rows` (Important finding)\n56\t\n57\t### What changed\n58\t- `rows.py` — added a guard at the top of `load_rows`: `if not name.isidentifier(): raise ValueError(f\"unsafe table name: {name!r}\")`, with a one-line comment explaining why interpolation is unavoidable (`?` binds values, not identifiers). Guard runs before the empty-`rows` early return, so an unsafe name is rejected even with no rows.\n59\t- `tests/test_rows.py` — added `test_load_rows_rejects_unsafe_table_name`, which asserts `ValueError` for the table name `\"t; DROP TABLE t\"`. Ruff's import sorter (I001) also regrouped `pytest` / `rows` / `tinycsv` imports; verified no import was dropped.\n60\t- Not touched (out of scope per fix brief): `header.py`, `schema.py`, and the two Minor findings (function docstring, untested empty-rows branch).\n61\t\n62\t### RED evidence (test added, fix not yet applied)\n63\t`uv run --no-project python -m pytest -q`\n64\t\n65\t```\n66\t        with pytest.raises(ValueError):\n67\t>           load_rows(conn, \"t; DROP TABLE t\", [[\"1\", \"2\"]])\n68\t\n69\ttests/test_rows.py:24:\n70\tconn = <sqlite3.Connection object at 0x7fa930448f40>, name = 't; DROP TABLE t'\n71\trows = [['1', '2']]\n72\t\n73\t>       conn.executemany(f\"INSERT INTO {name} VALUES ({placeholders})\", rows)\n74\tE       sqlite3.OperationalError: near \";\": syntax error\n75\t\n76\trows.py:8: OperationalError\n77\t=========================== short test summary info ============================\n78\tFAILED tests/test_rows.py::test_load_rows_rejects_unsafe_table_name - sqlite3...\n79\t1 failed, 2 passed in 0.05s\n80\t```\n81\t\n82\tThe failure mode itself confirms the finding: without the guard the raw name reached SQLite as SQL text.\n83\t\n84\t### GREEN evidence (full suite re-run after fix)\n85\t`uv run --no-project python -m pytest -q`\n86\t\n87\t```\n88\t...                                                                      [100%]\n89\t3 passed in 0.04s\n90\t```\n91\t\n92\tRuff was run on the touched files before committing (`ruff format`: 2 files left unchanged; `ruff check --fix`: 1 error fixed — the I001 import ordering noted above).\n93\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0174HA4CSrVYJZLEpbLXcnTz","session_id":"7183153f-7116-4943-8f43-18f1f1380ed3","uuid":"ab96c2eb-db2b-4926-bd74-da42c2708734","timestamp":"2026-07-30T17:20:34.111Z","subagent_type":"sdd-reviewer","task_description":"Re-review Task 3 after fix"}
