{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ab92da38f2643840d","tool_use_id":"toolu_0174HA4CSrVYJZLEpbLXcnTz","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-1000/-tmp-claude-1000--home-jacek-prjs-skills-269c2167-adeb-455e-8871-4018c0077d3f-scratchpad-probes-probeB-run/7183153f-7116-4943-8f43-18f1f1380ed3/tasks/ab92da38f2643840d.output","summary":"### Spec Compliance\nMatches the Task 3 brief: `load_rows(conn, name: str, rows: list[list[str]]) -> int` in `rows.py`, test-first in `tests/test_rows.py`, count of inserted rows returned. No missing/extra/misunderstood elements. The prior Important finding (unvalidated `name` interpolated into raw SQL) is resolved: `rows.py:6-7` now runs `if not name.isidentifier(): raise ValueError(...)` as the very first statement in `load_rows`, ahead of both the empty-`rows` early return and the `INSERT INTO {name} ...` f-string construction — so no code path builds or executes SQL with an unvalidated table name.\n\n### Strengths\n- Guard placement is correct: it fires before any SQL string is built, including on the empty-`rows` path, so there is no bypass.\n- New test `test_load_rows_rejects_unsafe_table_name` (`tests/test_rows.py:16-21`) passes a realistic injection-shaped string (`\"t; DROP TABLE t\"`), which genuinely fails `str.isidentifier()` — this is a real exercise of the rejection path, not a vacuous assert-always-true test.\n- Fix is surgical: only `rows.py` and `tests/test_rows.py` touched, no scope creep into sibling files.\n- One-line comment on the guard explains *why* interpolation is needed (`?` binds values, not identifiers) — useful non-obvious context, matches repo comment conventions.\n\n### Issues (Critical/Important/Minor)\n**Critical:** none.\n**Important:** none — prior finding verified fixed.\n**Minor:**\n- `load_rows` still has no function-level docstring (carried over from round 1, explicitly out of scope for this fix).\n- The `if not rows: return 0` branch is still untested (carried over from round 1, explicitly out of scope for this fix).\n- `str.isidentifier()` also accepts Python/SQL reserved words (e.g. `\"select\"`) and some Unicode identifiers that may not be valid unquoted SQLite identifiers — not a security gap (no injection surface), only a correctness edge case; not worth blocking on.\n\n### Assessment\n**Task quality:** Approved\n**Reasoning:** The guard runs before any SQL is constructed on every code path, and the new test exercises real rejection behavior rather than asserting a tautology — the Important finding is genuinely closed, with no new issues introduced by the fix commit.","usage":{"total_tokens":29937,"tool_uses":5,"duration_ms":43394},"uuid":"0d6c6a4c-331d-4143-bfc8-a6948127e4ea","session_id":"7183153f-7116-4943-8f43-18f1f1380ed3"}
